---
title: How to read the API
description: Find an app API, understand its signature, and recognize deeper framework material.
sidebar:
  order: 3
---

Use the reference while building something: choose the control or service you
need, try its example, then look up the relevant member. You do not need to learn
every base class, resource type or native interface first.

## Choose the level you need

| Your goal | Read |
| --- | --- |
| Build an ordinary app | Tutorials, layout/modules guides, concrete controls such as TextButton, and services your app uses. |
| Add reusable app behavior | Optional source libraries for commands, documents, preferences, JSON and numeric input. |
| Work directly with sockets, native requests or pixel buffers | Pages marked **Advanced app/host integration API**. These are public, but require more setup and careful cleanup. |
| Embed LUI or implement a backend/provider | The engineering documents and C/C++ ABI headers linked under deeper reading. They are not extra setup steps for SDK app authors. |

**Public** does not mean **available on every host**. A reflected signature can
exist while an optional implementation is unavailable. For example,
CodeEditor supports plain editing, but public SetStyles is refused in this SDK.
Each affected page states that limitation.

## Properties, methods and signals

```luau
Button.Text = "Save" -- Assign a property.
local Children = Window:GetChildren() -- Call a method on an object.
Button.Activated:Connect(function() -- Subscribe to a signal.
    Button.Text = "Saved"
end)
```

This fragment assumes an existing Window and TextButton. The
[TextButton reference](../../api/classes/textbutton/) supplies the complete app.

A **property** holds a value. A **method** performs an operation. A **signal**
notifies your code that something happened. The callback arguments differ by
signal: Activated has no arguments, while InputBegan passes an InputEvent.

`Button:Destroy()` destroys the button and its children and disconnects its
signals. A connection to a separate service, such as ThemeService, should be
disconnected when the UI using it is destroyed. An async operation created by
your app also needs its own cancellation/cleanup.

## Understand a signature

The generated type may show:

```luau
FindFirstChild: (Self: Instance, Name: string) -> Instance?
```

Call it as `Window:FindFirstChild("SaveButton")`. The colon supplies **Self**;
you do not pass Window twice. The result is an Instance or nil, so check it
before using it.

| Notation | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `string`, `number`, `boolean` | Ordinary Luau value types. |
| `T?` | A T value or nil. Check for nil when the value can be absent. |
| `{T}` | An array of T values. |
| `A \| B` | One of two possible types. Check a distinguishing field before reading type-specific fields. |
| `(boolean, string?)` | Two return values: a boolean and an optional string. Check the method’s explanation for what they mean. |
| `read Field: T` | A read-only field. You can read it but cannot assign it. |
| `Self` | The receiver supplied by a colon call. It is not an extra user argument. |
| `WindowInit` or another `Init` type | The table of starting properties passed to Instance.new. It is not a class to construct. |

For example, create `Instance.new("TextButton", {Text = "Save"})`, not
`Instance.new("TextButtonInit")`. The properties table does not replace the
button; it supplies its initial values.

## Inherited members

TextButton inherits from GuiButton, GuiObject and Instance. You can therefore
assign `Button.Size`, call `Button:Destroy()` and connect `Button.InputBegan`
even though those members are explained on base-class pages. Inherited-member
links take you to their descriptions. A non-creatable base such as GuiObject
exists to describe shared behavior; create a concrete control instead.

## Tasks and asynchronous calls

Methods such as HttpService:GetAsync wait for I/O and must run in a task:

```luau
Button.Activated:Connect(function()
    task.spawn(function()
        local Ok, Response = pcall(function()
            return app:GetService("HttpService"):GetAsync("http://127.0.0.1:8080/status")
        end)
        if Ok then
            Status.Text = "HTTP " .. Response.StatusCode
        else
            Status.Text = "Request failed"
        end
    end)
end)
```

This fragment assumes Button, Status, `network.client` and a running local
endpoint. The [HTTP reference](../../api/services/httpservice/) has a complete
entry file, setup and expected result. A completed HTTP 404 and a transport
failure are different outcomes. `pcall` handles a raised failure; check
`Response.Success` or StatusCode for the completed response.

Other services use callbacks instead of yielding: ClipboardService:ReadText,
for example, calls your function later. Follow the method’s own contract rather
than adding Async to a name or assuming every operation returns its result
immediately.

## Terms used in deeper pages

| Term | In ordinary app work |
| --- | --- |
| Host | The program running your Luau code, normally the SDK’s native runner. |
| Provider or adapter | The host component implementing an optional service. The SDK supplies supported adapters; you write one only for custom integration. |
| Generation | One run of your app’s Luau state. Restart creates a new one, so unsaved in-memory state is lost. |
| Retire | Stop using an old object/run and prevent its callbacks from affecting the replacement. |
| Receipt | A ticket tying an asynchronous document load/save to the exact document/revision it began with. A later edit can make it stale. |
| Admission | The runtime accepted an operation. It does not necessarily mean that I/O finished or pixels appeared on screen. |
| Storage scope | The app-private area associated with a project identity. It is not unrestricted access to the computer’s files. |
| Reflection | Metadata used to derive/check API names, types and reference pages. App code does not need to implement it. |

Full declarations and source links remain available for precision. They are
expandable deeper references, rather than a prerequisite for following examples.
